{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact head 84bfe52f68e0f5a21b33219fb5e756068dbd226e on request/batch-test-stdout, reporting artifact df30179c, governing request 9f697435 and promise 672b0fb9. One commit over main; `git diff --name-only main...84bfe52f` is cmd/gs/main_test.go alone, with Rests-On: 9f697435.\n\nArchitecture. Layer 7 (CLI, MCP, skills, connectors and UI) per docs/reference/architecture.md; no other layer is touched. The head preserves that layer's contract rather than changing it: machine output stays on stdout (printJSON, cmd/gs/main.go:3336-3340) and diagnostics stay on stderr, of which the batch path has four writers, not one: showResolved (cmd/gs/eventref.go:48-52), discloseBases (:94-103), noteDeadRestsOn (:117-129) and the slow-audit banner (cmd/gs/main.go:2491). The test harness was the only thing conflating the streams, and CombinedOutput was fragile against all four, so the fix removes the class of failure, not just the reported trigger. No production, kernel, fold, query, wire or authority change; no architecture-page update is owed.\n\nSecurity. No new trust or authority boundary. The child process is the test's own go build output at an absolute t.TempDir() path, run against disposable fixture repositories. The shell wrapper at cmd/gs/main_test.go:5063-5064 is safe argv, not a shell string: sh -c 'printf \"%s\\n\" \"$1\" \u003e\u00262; shift; exec \"$@\"' receives every path and flag as a positional parameter. I probed it with command substitution, a statement separator, a pipe, a glob and a space-bearing argument; all arrived verbatim, nothing expanded or executed, no file created. The literal-slice append allocates a fresh backing array so command.Args is not aliased; exec replaces the shell so the real exit status reaches command.Run(), and every failure path (run error, decode, report shape) quotes stderr. The two non-zero-exit controls at :5084 and :5099 and the real slow-audit test at :378 are unchanged and green. No untrusted input, secret, bound, signature or admission decision is involved; no new dependency. Authority facts from the durable record: request 9f697435 (requester planner a6ac0d54) to codex 5f12e916, promise 672b0fb9, report df30179c recognised on the commitment row as the report (awaiting-review). df30179c is live at cmd/gs/main_test.go at 84bfe52f, covering the whole diff; its staleness (stale_because eadab5d4) crosses the retired predecessor request only, inherited and disclosed in the request and the promise, and it carries no describes_superseded_world fact, so the merge records the staleness rather than refusing. Its one live predecessor 12ac9d0e at the same path is codex's own, so the exact-path retirement needs no second author.\n\nSimplification. One existing test gains one case; no helper framework, no production hook, no new dependency; nothing to cut. Two cosmetic observations that weaken no condition and do not warrant another round: withProgress is re-derived by string comparison against the loop value (:5058 vs :5046), where a small table or a []bool loop would keep name and flag from drifting; and the notice literal at :5057 duplicates the production wording without sharing a constant, so it reads as pinning that message when the fixture only needs some stderr. Keeping the plain positional-file case beside the progress case is correct: the negative control fails only on the new case while the plain one still passes, which is what proves the new case pins the defect. One parallel place remains outside scope, in production tooling: cmd/gitseq-perf/main.go:534-542 also feeds CombinedOutput into a JSON decoder; the request confined this head to same-cause test corrections, so that is a separate request if anyone wants it closed.\n\nWhat I ran at exact 84bfe52f in a detached read-only worktree, each under bash with pipefail: go vet ./cmd/gs/... rc 0; go build ./... rc 0; go test -count=1 -run 'BatchProcess|Batch' ./cmd/gs/... rc 0 (ok 119.077s); go test -count=1 -run BatchProcess ./cmd/gs/... rc 0 (ok 7.917s); go test -race -count=1 -run BatchProcess ./cmd/gs/... rc 0 (ok 4.545s); go test -count=3 -v -run TestBatchProcessReadsItsFileAndReportsFailures ./cmd/gs/ rc 0, four subtests passing on all three iterations; go test -count=1 -run TestSlowLocalAuditReportsProgressWithoutChangingTheResult ./cmd/gs/ rc 0; gofmt -l cmd/gs/ clean; the same focused batch set under 24 concurrent busy-loop processes on an 18-core machine rc 0 (ok 236.776s). git merge-tree --write-tree main 84bfe52f gives tree e301975fa1b52018e81c01485abe05180e2865f9 with no conflict against main 0afa98aa. Independent negative control in a git archive copy outside the repository: reverting the separation to a true CombinedOutput parse fails only positional_file_with_stderr_progress, at \"invalid character 'g' looking for beginning of value\" with the banner prefixed to the report, while positional_file still passes. The artifact's own control is the concatenation variant its text describes and yields \"after top-level value\"; both are accurate to their descriptions and prove the same proposition. Three runs of the mutant's plain case under the same CPU load all passed, so the load run corroborates rather than reproduces the original timing failure; the deterministic case is the proof, as the requester stated. Focused and race timings differ from the artifact's 9.422s and 17.500s only by build-cache warmth; nothing is relabelled.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:df30179cfab291467eb9eb4c8c2bfd9cce5287c3","binding":"assigned","head":"84bfe52f68e0f5a21b33219fb5e756068dbd226e","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:92676f56ff71cec76fb7ae3c04ab3accd70d8740\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:672b0fb90774ab01f25cd244baac5056d373ab8a\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:ec8bc512e19aa36